  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely from late \ntonight into the weekend. Forecast confidence on any particular \nweather system is low, but travelers should continue to monitor \nthis period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... Coastal low has developed off of \nthe SC/NC coast and is forecast to head inland today into the \narea of greatest pressure falls. This low pressure will track \njust inland of the eastern seaboard and spread precipitation \ninto the area around midnight tonight.\n\nBy the time precipitation arrives surface temperatures will\nlikely to have risen above freezing south of Rte 9, aka the\nAirline Rd. Areas from about Bangor east toward Princeton should\nbegin as rain and remain that way throughout the event. Having\nsaid that however, cannot rule out slick spots on bridges late\ntonight over interior Downeast. \n\nAs precipitation spreads northward of Bangor through daybreak \nsurface temperatures will be warming into the upper 20s leading \nto freezing rain in the morning with total ice amounts ranging \nfrom a glaze to as much as 0.10 inches by the time temperatures \ngo above freezing. There is potential in the northern areas for\nlocalized freezing drizzle as temperatures struggle to climb \nabove freezing. \n\nOnset time for the freezing rain just north of Bangor will be \n1-2am tomorrow with the precip reaching the upper most northern\nareas by 7-8am. With rough end timing for the freezing rain in \nareas just north of Bangor between 6-7am and change over to rain.\nAs more areas will change over to rain as the morning \nprogresses. Our most northern areas have a rough end time of 1pm\nor later with still the possibility of localized freezing \ndrizzle due to struggling surface temperatures.\n\nWrap around snow showers are expected over the northwest Friday\nnight into Saturday morning with rain showers expected over the\nremainder of nrn areas. Rain and snow accumulations are expected\nto be light with these showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous marine conditions and possible \ncoastal impacts around the time of the midday high tide on \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... The high tide 17z on Friday will \nbe within the top 20 of highest tide for the month of November. \nThe maximum surge values will be near the low tide on Friday \nmorning with a surge of 0.8 to 1 foot at Friday/s midday high \ntide. With seas of 6-12 feet and wave period of > 10s cannot \nrule out splashover along the roads closest to the shore.",
